  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of this evening is expected to remain mostly dry. Current \nsatellite and observational trends show the lake breeze boundary \nmake its way inland from the Saginaw Bay and southern half of Lake \nHuron. There is a low chance (<15%) for a rogue shower to develop \nalong the lake breeze boundary through the evening hours.\n\nThe area of high pressure which has brought a bout of nice weather \nover these last few days will gradually weaken and move over the Mid-\nAtlantic region Friday through Saturday, in response to an \napproaching and deepening longwave trough moving in from the \nwest. This change from large scale subsidence to what looks to \nbe multiple rounds of ascent moving into the region will transition \nour weather pattern to a more active one for this weekend. Friday \nwill be a transition day where we will see an uptick in cloud cover, \nbut most of the day should remain dry. \n\nThe forecast for Friday night through Sunday will hinge heavily on \nhow a slow-moving upper level trough, the parent surface low \ntrack across the Midwest, and whether or not this upper level wave \nphases with another lobe of energy digging south into the region on \nSunday. Chances for precipitation for Saturday and now Sunday have \nincreased with this forecast package update, but this is still\nconsiderable uncertainty in the weekend forecast. \n\nMost ensemble members show a considerable increase in PWATs ahead of\nthe main upper level wave as moisture and warm air advection track \ninto the region and wrap back around the surface low, progged to \ntrack across Illinois and Indiana. If this track holds true, then we \nwill be in a moisture-rich environment supportive of heavy rain, with\nmultiple rounds of large-scale ascent and an increased risk for\nepisodes of training convection. The drier solutions feature a \nmore open trough and a surface low that is considerably further \nsouth. This uncertainty leaves a wide range of possible outcomes for \nrain and possible impacts across southeast Michigan. If the higher \nend rainfall solutions come to fruition, flooding may become a \nconcern in urban areas, especially Saturday night and possibly \nSunday. \n\nBy Monday, high pressure should settle back into the region as large-\nscale subsidence takes over and a narrow, yet amplified upper level \nridge moves into the region. This will favor a warming and drying \ntrend into early next week.",
